WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
Hey, Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:06.540
One of the greatest myths of childhood is that&nbsp;
when we grow up, we’ll finally be in charge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.100
But while it’s true that reaching adulthood&nbsp;
means you get to choose our own bedtime and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:16.560
eat pizza for dinner every night, the idea&nbsp;
that we suddenly become masters of our own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.740
destinies is simply an illusion - whether it’s&nbsp;
paying taxes or staying at home during lockdown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:28.320
sometimes we all have to do things we don’t&nbsp;
want to do. Still, aside from the rules and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:33.480
regulations passed down by the government,&nbsp;
our employers, (...and our partners),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.220
we like to believe that our decisions&nbsp;
are ours - and ours alone - to make.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:43.500
But even that’s far from a sure thing&nbsp;
- philosophers and thinkers have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:48.540
debating whether or not we truly have&nbsp;
free will ever since some guy in a toga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.680
first wondered why on earth he drank&nbsp;
that third flagon of wine last night.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.320
Don’t worry, I’m not going to open that&nbsp;
philosophical can of worms here - I have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:03.120
another whole video dedicated to the topic of&nbsp;
free will - but suffice to say humans are quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:07.560
attached to the notion of being autonomous&nbsp;
beings that get to choose their path in life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:13.800
and it’s not hard to see why. To believe the&nbsp;
opposite is to accept that our futures have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:20.640
already been laid out before us, and that&nbsp;
we’re mere NPCs rather than active players.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:27.060
You might even say we’d be no better than&nbsp;
zombies, which would actually be quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:34.800
handy because then I could casually segue into&nbsp;
the topic of today’s video, real-life zombies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.580
Now, if you’re going to be a scientific wet&nbsp;
blanket and declare that there are no such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:51.120
things as zombies, I suggest you visit Ikea&nbsp;
on a Saturday afternoon and see for yourself.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.760
Pay close attention to the faces of men aimlessly&nbsp;
pushing trolleys, nodding and smiling when asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.480
whether they prefer the SKOGSKORN scatter cushion&nbsp;
or the TUVSÄV. Notice the emptiness in their eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:14.880
their vacant expressions, and the way they follow&nbsp;
the arrows of flatpack doom as if slaves to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.940
unseen force. Admittedly that force is probably&nbsp;
the promise of Swedish meatballs and a slice of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.740
Daim cake rather than the chance to feast&nbsp;
on the brains of the living, but still.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:31.980
Of course, it isn’t just really big Scandinavian&nbsp;
furniture shops that have the power to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:37.800
living beings into mindless zombies - that&nbsp;
utter sadist Mother Nature is pretty good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.080
at it too. Because real life zombies are&nbsp;
surprisingly common in the natural world.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.420
Take, for example, Plesiometa argyra.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:56.040
This poor spider is routinely chosen to play an&nbsp;
unwitting and unhappy support role raising the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:02.160
young of a certain parasitic wasp who’s breeding&nbsp;
habits read like something out of a horror film.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:08.220
The wasp starts by paralysing the spider&nbsp;
with a single sting before laying her larvae&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:13.020
on the surface of its body. Along&nbsp;
with the unwanted babysitting gig,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:18.000
the spider receives another sinister&nbsp;
surprise - brand-new biological coding.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.780
The wasp’s larvae begin to grow, sucking on the&nbsp;
spider’s bodily fluids through tiny puncture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:30.780
holes they make in its abdomen. To begin with,&nbsp;
the spider basically just goes about its regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:37.680
spidery business as if a host of tiny parasites&nbsp;
aren’t making milkshake out of its insides.

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:43.680
But after two weeks, something changes.&nbsp;
Through a mechanism we still don’t understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:48.060
the spider will suddenly stop&nbsp;
tending to its large circular web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:54.840
and will instead feel compelled to craft a&nbsp;
small, sturdy platform unlike any web it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:00.600
ever woven. The spider will spend the last night&nbsp;
of its life on this crazed construction project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:08.880
and its reward for finishing is to be eaten alive&nbsp;
by the hungry larvae hanging out on its abdomen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.040
With that final nutritious meal in their&nbsp;
bellies, the larvae will begin spinning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:20.040
their cocoons, which they neatly hang&nbsp;
from their bespoke spiderweb scaffold.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:27.120
Another real-life zombie is the woodlouse, also&nbsp;
known as the pill bug, a common household critter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:34.620
with a fatal fetish for faeces. This little poo&nbsp;
nibbler has a particular thing for bird droppings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:40.920
including those deposited by starlings.&nbsp;
Unfortunately for the pill bug, starling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:46.320
digestive tracts often carry a parasite that’s far&nbsp;
more dangerous to the bug than it is to the bird.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:54.000
Once safely ensconced inside the pill bug, the&nbsp;
parasite seizes control. To complete its life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:06:01.080
cycle, it needs to make its way back inside a&nbsp;
starling, and so it compels the woodlouse to seek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:07.920
out the most exposed location it can find. Nothing&nbsp;
is as tantalising to starling tastebuds as a pill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.920
bug out in the open dancing the macarena, so the&nbsp;
insect is soon gobbled up, and the parasite goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:21.420
back to colonising the gut of its original host.&nbsp;
Clever. Disgusting and horrifying, but clever.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:28.800
The green-banded broodsac, uses similar poo-based&nbsp;
tactics, only this time it’s a species of snail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:34.680
that eats the bird’s faeces. It’s well known that&nbsp;
snails are absolutely terrible at the macarena,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.640
so the broodsac has another way of attracting&nbsp;
the interest of the local bird population - it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:47.280
grows directly into the snail’s eyestalks,&nbsp;
where it begins to throb and pulsate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.940
doing a surprisingly good impression of&nbsp;
a catapilar. Before you can say ‘Simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:58.980
Simon,’ in swoops a bird to gobble up the&nbsp;
snail… and the broodsac along with it.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:06.660
As horrific as all these examples are,&nbsp;
nothing strikes as much undead fear as fungi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.120
The natural world is made up of multiple&nbsp;
kingdoms, with the most well-known being Plants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.120 --> 00:07:19.140
Animals and Narnia. Fungi, which include yeasts,&nbsp;
moulds, and mushrooms, have a kingdom all to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.620
themselves and judging by the name, it’s&nbsp;
where you want to go to have a good time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:27.420
There are, of course, many different types&nbsp;
of fungi that do many different things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:32.880
including entomopathogenic fungi,&nbsp;
that infect (and often kill) insects.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:39.960
One such fungus is Beauveria bassiana that&nbsp;
can be found growing in soils all over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:46.320
world. It infects insects and other arthropods by&nbsp;
penetrating their exoskeletons and growing inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:52.920
them, killing them within a few days. If that was&nbsp;
all this fatal fungus did I’d consider it to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:59.040
pretty bloody rude, but no. Beauveria bassiana&nbsp;
then proceeds to colonise the poor insect’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:05.400
corpse, its spores appearing all over the dead&nbsp;
body before drifting off into the air in search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:13.080
of more stuff to murder. Beauveria bassiana is&nbsp;
so good at wiping out certain types of insects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:20.280
it’s actually used as a biological insecticide to&nbsp;
control bugs like termites, aphids, and beetles.

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:27.420
It’s safe to say Beauveria bassiana behaves&nbsp;
brutally, but as far as Entomopathogenic fungi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:33.120
go it’s arguably one of the friendlier&nbsp;
ones. A particular genus of fungi known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:41.160
as cordyceps found worldwide in tropical and&nbsp;
temperate regions has a very particular skill:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:47.880
it doesn’t just kill its hosts, it first releases&nbsp;
chemicals into their brains, taking control of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:56.520
their bodies and turning them into automatons.&nbsp;
No wonder it’s known as ‘zombie fungi’. No one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.520 --> 00:09:02.400
knows exactly how many cordyceps species there&nbsp;
are, but there are at least 600, and most target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:07.140
a single species of insect - anything from&nbsp;
butterflies to dragonflies and cockroaches.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:15.480
Ophiocordyceps curculionum attacks weevils in&nbsp;
Central and South America. Ophiocordyceps sinensis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:21.960
kills caterpillars up in the Himalayas. And&nbsp;
ophiocordyceps humbertii infects wasps in Brazil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:28.440
causing them to land on a leaf or branch&nbsp;
and bite down into it before passing away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:35.940
This peculiar behaviour is also seen in the&nbsp;
effects of the ophiocordyceps unilateralis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:41.460
probably the most famous of the cordyceps&nbsp;
fungi and scourge of ants across the globe.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:48.120
Like its fellow zombie fungi, unilateralis&nbsp;
gets inside its victim using special enzymes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:53.100
that dissolve chitin, the fibrous substance&nbsp;
that makes up most arthropod exoskeletons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:59.460
Once inside, the fungus takes control of the&nbsp;
ant’s brain, making it climb high into a tree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:07.260
where it bites down on a leaf and pops its six&nbsp;
tiny clogs. Soon, the fungus begins to bloom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:14.520
growing out through the ant’s exoskeleton into&nbsp;
balls of spores that burst and shower down on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:20.520
all the ants scurrying across the forest floor&nbsp;
below, starting the process all over again.

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:29.580
It’s a terrifying mode of operation,&nbsp;
but it’s also very successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:36.900
because cordyceps have been playing this game for&nbsp;
a very very long time. Fossil records show zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:43.260
ants controlled by cordyceps-like fungus were&nbsp;
taking their last bite into a leaf at least 48&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:50.280
million years ago. Other researchers have found&nbsp;
a fungus-infected insect stuck in amber that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:57.060
goes back more than 100 million years. Part of the&nbsp;
reason for this success is fungi’s adaptability.

00:10:57.060 --> 00:11:02.820
Scientists recently discovered that ants are&nbsp;
sometimes colonised by more than one species of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:07.020
cordyceps. Originally it looked like the two&nbsp;
different parasites were living in harmony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:11.700
but further research suggests that this&nbsp;
is probably an example of hyperparasitism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:19.740
a situation in which one parasite becomes infected&nbsp;
by another. Remember I said earlier that fungi eat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:26.280
into insect’s bodies by dissolving the chitin&nbsp;
in their exoskeletons? Well, guess what also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:34.380
contains plenty of chitin? Fungi! So, in another&nbsp;
weird turn, fungi are practically designed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:42.240
infect each other, which is why some cordyceps&nbsp;
cannibals specialise in attacking other cordyceps.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:49.620
So, we’re talking about a hyper-adaptable&nbsp;
organism that can colonise something thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:56.700
of times bigger than it, taking over its mind and&nbsp;
forcing it into some form of ritualistic suicide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.440 --> 00:12:01.500
By now there should only be&nbsp;
one question on your mind…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:07.440
but don’t worry, there’s no reason to&nbsp;
believe humans can be infected by an as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.360
yet undiscovered fungus and turned into&nbsp;
real life Clickers from The Last of Us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:19.440
In fact, it’s kind of the opposite - we may be&nbsp;
able to use these fatal fungi to our advantage.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:26.100
Cordyceps fungi contain a chemical called&nbsp;
cordycepin that, in biological organisms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:32.700
switches on a cellular protein called AMPK.&nbsp;
And it turns out AMPK is pretty powerful stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:38.400
Initial tests suggest drugs that trigger AMPK&nbsp;
production in our bodies are able to reverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:44.700
some types of diabetes and cancer, limit&nbsp;
heart disease, and even make us live longer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:51.240
Now scientists think they’ve found a way to grow&nbsp;
cordyceps fungi effectively in laboratories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:56.940
potentially opening up the way for new&nbsp;
anti-viral and cancer-fighting wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.940 --> 00:13:03.900
drugs based on cordycepin. Of course, it might&nbsp;
be a hard pill to swallow when you know the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:11.160
medicine you’re taking is made from a zombie&nbsp;
fungus. Then again, maybe it’ll grow on you.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:14.940
Thanks for watching.

